## Proof

**Remark on hypotheses.** The statement as written requires $f$ to be continuous; this is the standard hypothesis under which the result holds (without continuity the claim is false — one can construct a map on $\mathbb{R}$ with a unique fixed point $0$ yet with an orbit $2, \tfrac{3}{2}, \tfrac{4}{3},\dots\to 1\notin\mathrm{Fix}(f)$). The proof below uses continuity of $f$, which is the intended assumption.

---

Let $L:=\lim_{n\to\infty}f^{n}(y)$. We must show $L=x_{0}$.

**Step 1 — The shifted sequence has the same limit.**  
The sequence $\bigl(f^{n+1}(y)\bigr)_{n\ge 0}$ is obtained from $\bigl(f^{n}(y)\bigr)_{n\ge 0}$ by deleting the single initial term $f^{0}(y)=y$. Deleting finitely many terms does not change convergence or the limit, so

$$
\lim_{n\to\infty}f^{n+1}(y)=L. \tag{1}
$$

**Step 2 — $L$ is a fixed point of $f$.**  
By definition of iterates, $f^{n+1}(y)=f\!\bigl(f^{n}(y)\bigr)$. Since $f$ is continuous and $f^{n}(y)\to L$, continuity allows us to interchange $f$ with the limit:

$$
f(L)=f\!\Bigl(\lim_{n\to\infty}f^{n}(y)\Bigr)=\lim_{n\to\infty}f\!\bigl(f^{n}(y)\bigr)=\lim_{n\to\infty}f^{n+1}(y)\stackrel{(1)}{=}L.
$$

Hence $f(L)=L$, i.e. $L\in\mathrm{Fix}(f)$.

**Step 3 — Uniqueness forces $L=x_{0}$.**  
By hypothesis $f$ has a **unique** fixed point $x_{0}$, so $\mathrm{Fix}(f)=\{x_{0}\}$. Since $L$ is a fixed point, we conclude

$$
L=x_{0}.
$$

Therefore $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}f^{n}(y)=x_{0}$, as was to be shown. $\blacksquare$

